%I A398559 #55 Sep 18 2026 06:45:51
%S A398559 247889,201973,162359,128489,99829,75869,56123,40129,27449,17669,
%T A398559 10399,5273,1949,109,-541,-271,673,2069,3719,5449,7109,8573,9739,
%U A398559 10529,10889,10789,10223,9209,7789,6029,4019,1873,-271,-2251,-3881,-4951,-5227,-4451,-2341
%N A398559 a(n) = n^4 - 99*n^3 + 3441*n^2 - 49259*n + 247889, for n >= 0.
%C A398559 The first 50 terms, a(0) to a(49), are all primes or negatives of primes. There are 49 distinct primes; the term -271 appears twice (at n = 15 and n = 32).
%C A398559 a(n) = B(49 - n), where B(x) = x^4 - 97*x^3 + 3294*x^2 - 45458*x + 213589 is the polynomial found by Mark Beyleveld in the 2006 Al Zimmermann Prime Generating Polynomials contest, the best result for quartics with integer coefficients: 50 successive values that are primes or negatives of primes, 49 distinct in absolute value. See the Ed Pegg, Jr. link.
%H A398559 Hilko Koning, <a href="/A398559/b398559.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..10000</a>
%H A398559 François Dress and Michel Olivier, <a href="https://projecteuclid.org/journals/experimental-mathematics/volume-8/issue-4/Polyn%c3%b4mes-prenant-des-valeurs-premi%c3%a8res/em/1047262355.full">Polynômes prenant des valeurs premières</a>, Experimental Mathematics, Volume 8, Issue 4 (1999), pages 319-338.
%H A398559 Ed Pegg, Jr., <a href="https://www.mathpuzzle.com/MAA/48-Prime%20Generating%20Polynomials/mathgames_07_17_06.html">Prime Generating Polynomials</a>, Math Games, Mathematical Association of America, Jul 17 2006.
%H A398559 Eric Weisstein's World of Mathematics, <a href="https://mathworld.wolfram.com/Prime-GeneratingPolynomial.html">Prime-Generating Polynomial</a>.
%H A398559 <a href="/index/Rec#order_05">Index entries for linear recurrences with constant coefficients</a>, signature (5,-10,10,-5,1).
%F A398559 From _Jianing Song_, Sep 18 2026: (Start)
%F A398559 G.f.: (247889 - 1037472*x + 1631384*x^2 - 1142466*x^3 + 300689*x^4)/(1 - x)^5.
%F A398559 E.g.f.: (247889 - 45916*x + 3151*x^2 - 93*x^3 + x^4)*exp(x). (End)
%e A398559 a(3) = 3^4 - 99*3^3 + 3441*3^2 - 49259*3 + 247889 = 128489, which is prime.
%t A398559 Table[n^4 - 99 n^3 + 3441 n^2 - 49259 n + 247889, {n, 0, 49}]
